{"subject":"Forensic Medicine","sourceTitle":"All In One Online","title":"PrepLadder · 23.Previous Year Questions","deckName":"created by tele @Neet_pg_bot::prep::Qbank::Forensic_Medicine::23.Previous_Year_Questions","questions":[{"id":"all-in-one-1760436516410","topic":"PrepLadder · 23.Previous Year Questions","number":1,"stem":"Marshall's triad (punctate lacerations, bruises, and abrasions) is seen in which type of blast injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primary blast injury B) Secondary blast injury C) Tertiary blast injury D) Quaternary blast injury"},{"label":"B","text":"Secondary blast injury"},{"label":"C","text":"Tertiary blast injury"},{"label":"D","text":"Quaternary blast injury"}],"answer":"B","answerText":"Secondary blast injury","solutionHtml":"<p>Secondary blast injuries are Caused by flying debris and shrapnel propelled by the blast. This results in <strong>Marshall's triad: <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"77074d46b51692777196747de93965\">punctate</span> lacerations, bruises, and abrasions</strong>, characteristic of injuries from <strong>high-velocity fragments.</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Blast Injury: </strong>Blast injuries are complex physical traumas resulting from direct or <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"2f73e160ba1692777190d65065c95b\">indirect</span> exposure to an explosion, leading to sudden changes in atmospheric pressure.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Classification of Blast Injuries:</strong></p>\n<table cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse; width:641px\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Type</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Mechanism</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Features</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Primary Injury</strong></p>\n<p><strong>(Option A ruled out)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>It results from blast waves and is often internal with no external signs.</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ears:</strong> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"391176370016927772011d1b8315bc\">Tympanic</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"34cd707428169277719272942b7905\">membrane</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e73553a3a5169277719787fd5f4680\">rupture</span> or <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a1b8deb0fe1692777190df5ce46525\">inner ear</span> disruption.</li>\n<li><strong>Lungs: Alveolar-capillary <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"34cd707428169277719272942b7905\">membrane</span> haemorrhage (blast lung), most common cause <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"254dc5106d1692777183ba9ab1b3e5\">cause of death</span> in survivors.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>GIT:</strong> Injuries to gas-filled organs are more common in underwater blasts.</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Secondary Injury</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>It is resulting from shrapnel, debris &amp; flying objects (propelled by explosion).</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li>Penetrating trauma.</li>\n<li><strong>The characteristic Marshall triad, which includes bruises, abrasions, and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"77074d46b51692777196747de93965\">punctate</span> lacerations, is evident.</strong></li>\n<li>Commonly cause severe <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"3c6021096816927772007954fcc158\">thoracic</span> injuries, including lacerations of the heart and great vessels.</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Tertiary Injury</strong></p>\n<p><strong>(Option C ruled out)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Victims are thrown against solid objects by blast winds.</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li>Blunt/Penetrating trauma.</li>\n<li>Includes bone fractures and coup-contrecoup injuries.</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Quaternary Injury</strong></p>\n<p><strong>(Option D ruled out)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Encompass all other injuries not classified above</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Flash burns</strong> from extreme heat.</li>\n<li><strong>Crush injuries</strong> and falls due to explosions.</li>\n<li><strong>Respiratory injuries</strong> from toxic dust, gases, or radiation exposure.</li>\n<li><strong>Psychiatric issues,</strong> such as <strong>post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD),</strong> may affect individuals even if they are physically unharmed.</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Gautam Biswas Review of Forensic Medicine and Toxicology, 3rd Edition, Page 290,291</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436516412","topic":"PrepLadder · 23.Previous Year Questions","number":2,"stemHtml":"Identify the source of the trauma given in the image below.<br/><img src=\"images/image_105_142_1.jpg\"/>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bullet"},{"label":"B","text":"Rod"},{"label":"C","text":"Axe"},{"label":"D","text":"Scissor"}],"answer":"A","answerText":"Bullet","solutionHtml":"<p><strong>Correct Option A- Bullet:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The above image is of Bullet wound showing the bevelling of the skull.</li>\n<li>Entry wound- Clean wound</li>\n<li>Exit wound- Cone-shaped erosion</li>\n<li>Bevelling is always seen at the exit point of the table</li>\n<li>In the entry wound, beveling is towards the inner table.</li>\n<li>In the exit wound, bevelling is towards the outer table</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Incorrect Options:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option B - Rod:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rod causes <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"324b1bfa7816927771834f9e3f6704\">blunt</span> fracture.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Option C - Axe:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Axe causes a chop wound.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Option D - Scissor:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Scissors causes a <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"13e924e3cb169277719021021fc6d2\">incised</span> wounds</li>\n</ul>\n<br/><br/>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436516414","topic":"PrepLadder · 23.Previous Year Questions","number":3,"stem":"What causes the blackening around a gunshot entry wound?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Smoke particles"},{"label":"B","text":"Unburned gunpowder"},{"label":"C","text":"Muzzle of firearm"},{"label":"D","text":"Fire effect"}],"answer":"A","answerText":"Smoke particles","solutionHtml":"trong&amp;gt;\n<ul>\n<li>The blackening is caused by <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"8d84f9df0c1692777198c9fee0fc57\">soot</span> or smoke particles from the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"260cad33301692777184652a5acd7d\">combustion</span> of gunpowder, which adhere to the skin around the entry wound. This phenomenon is typically observed in close-range gunshot wounds.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Incorrect Options:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Unburned gunpowder (Option B): </strong>Tattooing or <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"37d5ea489a1692777199ec08c05fe6\">stippling</span> results from the impact of partially burned or unburned gunpowder particles on the skin. These cause small reddish-brown or black marks but do not lead to blackening.</p>\n<p><strong>Muzzle of firearm (Option C):</strong> The <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a36e89561c16927771933be65f4967\">muzzle</span> may leave an <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"522f9728161692777189dac58f3dfd\">imprint</span> if the firearm is discharged at close or contact range, but this does not cause the blackening around the wound.</p>\n<p><strong>Fire effect (Option D): </strong>The flame from the firearm may produce <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e316517c941692777200eb2671128d\">thermal</span> burns near the wound, but this is distinct from the blackening caused by smoke particles.</p><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">KS Narayan Reddy and OP Murthy Essentials of Forensic Medicine and Toxicology, 35th edition, Page 164, 165, 166, 167.<br/>Gautam Biswas Review of Forensic Medicine and Toxicology, 3rd Edition, Page 218, 219.</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436516416","topic":"PrepLadder · 23.Previous Year Questions","number":4,"stem":"Bullet fingerprinting involve:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primary markings"},{"label":"B","text":"Secondary markings"},{"label":"C","text":"Both"},{"label":"D","text":"None"}],"answer":"C","answerText":"Both","solutionHtml":"arkings on bullets include the <strong>number, diameter, and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"1b0d31fe811692777202644090ba60\">width</span> of lands and grooves (striations)</strong> and the <strong>degree of rifling twist</strong>.\n<li><strong>Surface irregularities</strong> serve as <strong>secondary markings.</strong></li>\n<li>These markings allow <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"d505e5aff31692777203af0565576c\">forensic</span> experts to connect bullets from a crime scene to a specific firearm, aiding in criminal investigations.</li>\n\n<table cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Primary Markings (Class characteristics)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Secondary Markings (Individual or <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"fb7ff97e071692777180252299cf10\">accidental</span> characteristics)</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li>Determined by manufacturing specifications, design, and dimensions are most useful for identifying the make and model of the firearm involved.</li>\n<li>It consists of:\n\t\t\t\t<ul>\n<li>Number, <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"afb15e4a331692777186e495b370a1\">diameter</span> and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"1b0d31fe811692777202644090ba60\">width</span> of lands and grooves</li>\n<li>Depth of grooves</li>\n<li>Direction and degree of rifling twist</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Surface irregularities</strong> on the bullet result from imperfections in the barrel and <strong>the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"2b1931a185169277718065e3fa42dd\">adhesion</span> of bullet particles to the bore during <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"0c2d898b0b1692777202c5d43d5e78\">firing</span> (Metallic Fouling)</strong>.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>It may be <strong>identical</strong> for bullets fired by <strong>two different weapons.</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>These are different for bullets fired by two different weapons, reflecting each firearm's distinct barrel characteristics.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>It's more pronounced for jacketed bullets.</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>It is more pronounced when the grooves deeply mark the lead bullet.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><br/>\nThe bullet found in the body, called the <strong>crime bullet</strong>, is compared under a <strong>Comparison Microscope</strong> with a <strong>test bullet</strong> fired from a suspected weapon (using the same brand and type of ammunition).</p><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Essentials of Forensic Medicine and Toxicology, KS Narayan Reddy, 35th Edition, Page 180.<br/>Gautam Biswas Review of Forensic Medicine and Toxicology, 3rd Edition, Page 226, 227.</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436516418","topic":"PrepLadder · 23.Previous Year Questions","number":5,"stemHtml":"Identify the injury depicted in the image provided.<br/><img src=\"images/image_126_72_1.jpg\"/>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Shot gun wound"},{"label":"B","text":"Tattooing"},{"label":"C","text":"Arsenic poisoning"},{"label":"D","text":"Graze wound"}],"answer":"A","answerText":"Shot gun wound","solutionHtml":"holes, <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"863f5457381692777190f05632a8b2\">irregular</span> wound edges, and tissue destruction is characteristic of a shotgun wound.\n\n<table cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"5\" style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><strong>Type of shotgun wounds</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Type of wound</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Contact</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Close</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Short/ Intermediate-range </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Distant </strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Entry wound</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Cruciate/Stellate (Skull) or</p>\n<p>Circular</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Circular</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Rat-hole like</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Multiple small <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"3f8b73c2b51692777195a76ca589cf\">pellet</span> wounds with the mostly dispersed entry wound</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Border of wound</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Contused, <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"863f5457381692777190f05632a8b2\">Irregular</span> and <strong>muzzled appearance</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Clean-cut, <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"863f5457381692777190f05632a8b2\">irregular</span> or lacerated</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Abraded and scalloped borders</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Irregular</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Blackening</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td rowspan=\"3\" style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Present around the margins and even inside the wound.</p>\n</td>\n<td rowspan=\"3\" style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Present</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Absent</p>\n</td>\n<td rowspan=\"3\" style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Absent</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Burning</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Absent</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Tattooing</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Present</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Images</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"198\" src=\"images/exp_126_72_1.jpg\" width=\"198\"/></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<div style=\"text-align:center\">\n<figure class=\"caption_element\" style=\"display:inline-block\"><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"Lorenzo Gitto, M.D., Robert Stoppacher, M.D.\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"NA\" data-source=\"https://www.pathologyoutlines.com/topic/forensicsgunshotwounds.html\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"140\" src=\"images/exp_126_72_2.jpg\" width=\"163\"/>\n<figcaption>{{caption_text}}</figcaption>\n</figure>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<div style=\"text-align:center\">\n<figure class=\"caption_element\" style=\"display:inline-block\"><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"Joseph Harrington\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"NA\" data-source=\"https://www.acepnow.com/article/the-clinical-forensic-evaluation-of-gunshot-wounds-in-the-ed/acep_0122-pg20e/\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"150\" src=\"images/exp_126_72_3.jpg\" width=\"211\"/>\n<figcaption>{{caption_text}}</figcaption>\n</figure>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<div style=\"text-align:center\">\n<figure class=\"caption_element\" style=\"display:inline-block\"><img alt=\"\" data-author='\"DJ Wilks dwilks@nhs.net Y Mirza G Bourke\"' data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"NA\" data-source=\"https://publishing.rcseng.ac.uk/doi/10.1308/rcsann.2014.96.5.396\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"171\" src=\"images/exp_126_72_4.jpg\" width=\"161\"/>\n<figcaption>{{caption_text}}</figcaption>\n</figure>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"413\" src=\"images/exp_126_72_5.jpg\" width=\"413\"/></p>\n<p><strong>Incorrect Options:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Tattooing (Option B):</strong> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"16777b606e16927772006cbb6869ce\">Tattooing</span> refers to pinpoint marks caused by unburned gunpowder particles <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"671bee44331692777187b1efe07164\">embedding</span> in the skin around the entry wound, typically seen in close-range firearm injuries. While <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"16777b606e16927772006cbb6869ce\">tattooing</span> can be a feature of shotgun wounds, it is not the primary injury type depicted.</p>\n<p><strong>Arsenic <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"072b8af16e1692777196f21b086f92\">poisoning</span> (Option C):</strong> Arsenic <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"072b8af16e1692777196f21b086f92\">poisoning</span> presents with <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"8f2e7fd96116927771995b451b5c90\">systemic</span> signs like Mee's lines (white lines on nails), <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b3a647ce2516927771881427f56c1d\">gastrointestinal</span> distress, and multi-organ failure. No external wound patterns are associated.</p>\n<p><strong>Graze wound (Option D):</strong> A graze wound occurs when a projectile or object scrapes the skin surface, leaving an elongated abrasion.</p>\n<p> </p><br/><img src=\"images/exp_126_72_1.jpg\"/><br/><img src=\"images/exp_126_72_2.jpg\"/><br/><img src=\"images/exp_126_72_3.jpg\"/><br/><img src=\"images/exp_126_72_4.jpg\"/><br/><img src=\"images/exp_126_72_5.jpg\"/><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">KS Narayan Reddy and OP Murthy Essentials of Forensic Medicine and Toxicology, 35th edition, Page 164, 165, 166, 167.<br/>Gautam Biswas Review of Forensic Medicine and Toxicology, 3rd Edition, Page 218, 219.</div>","tags":""}]}